\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} = ky(a-y)\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n$$\ny(t) = \\dfrac{a}{1+Ce^{-akt}}\n$$\n\nwhere $C>0$ is an integration constant. Let's now derive this formula.\n\nOur differential equation is separable, so we start by separating variables and then integrating with respect to $t\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dt} & =ky(a-y) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y(a-y)}\\frac{dy}{dt} & =k \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{y(a-y)}\\frac{dy}{dt}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int k\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{y(a-y)}\\,dy & =\\int k\\,dt\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the fact that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{ 1}{y(a-y)} = \\dfrac{1}{a}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{y} + \\dfrac{1}{a-y}\\right),\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite the integrand on the left-hand side and complete the integration, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{y(a-y)}\\,dy & =\\int k\\,dt \\\\ \\frac{1}{a}\\int (\\frac{1}{y}+\\frac{1}{a-y})\\,dy & =\\int k\\,dt \\\\ \\int (\\frac{1}{y}-\\frac{1}{y-a})\\,dy & =ak\\int \\,dt \\\\ -\\int (\\frac{1}{y-a}-\\frac{1}{y})\\,dy & =ak\\int \\,dt \\\\ \\int (\\frac{1}{y-a}-\\frac{1}{y})\\,dy & =-ak\\int \\,dt \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}|y-a|-\\operatorname{ln}|y| & =-akt+{C}_{1} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}|\\frac{y-a}{y}| & =-akt+{C}_{1} \\\\ |\\frac{y-a}{y}| & ={e}^{-akt+{C}_{1}} \\\\ |\\frac{y-a}{y}| & ={e}^{{C}_{1}}{e}^{-akt} \\\\ \\frac{y-a}{y} & =\\pm {e}^{{C}_{1}}{e}^{-akt} \\\\ \\frac{y-a}{y} & =\\pm C{e}^{-akt}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=e^{C_1}$ is a positive constant. \n\nIn our modeling, we have assumed that $0 < y  < a.$ Therefore, the left-hand side of the equation must be negative since the numerator is negative and the denominator is positive. Therefore, we discard the positive option, and we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{y-a}{y}  =  -C e^{-akt}.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y-a}{y} & =-C{e}^{-akt} \\\\ y-a & =-Cy{e}^{-akt} \\\\ y+y\\cdot C{e}^{-akt} & =a \\\\ y(1+C{e}^{-akt}) & =a \\\\ y & =\\frac{a}{1+C{e}^{-akt}}\\,\\,\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180682 #edb/ref 1065 4521 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180682 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "ba0b74de-5c49-5d2e-a9a6-207220dbb90e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180682 #edb/ref 1067 "Deriving the General Solution" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180792 #edb/ref 1064 "Recall that a differential equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm dy}{\\mathrm dt} = ky(a-y)\n$$\n\nis called a logistic growth equation. The positive constant $a > 0$ is the carrying capacity, and $k > 0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nA typical solution curve $y = y(t)$ to this equation is shown below. \n\n![](images/0f/0f416479bbf72d3b9c1bf48861883e0e77bbc1c1d60d7fa48fb89e6f21c104eb.png)\n\nIt can be shown that the general solution to this equation is given by\n\n$$\ny(t) = \\frac{a}{1+C e^{-ak t}}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an integration constant. \n\nNote the following properties of this general solution:\n\n- Since $a$ and $k$ are both positive, $e^{-akt}\\to0$ as $t\\to\\infty.$ Therefore, $y(t)\\to a$ as $t\\to\\infty.$ In other words, $y(t)$ approaches the carrying capacity as $t$ increases. This agrees with the behavior shown in our diagram.\n\n- Since $e^{-akt}\\to\\infty$ as $t\\to-\\infty,$ we have that $y(t)\\to 0$ as $t\\to-\\infty.$ This also agrees with our diagram.\n\nWe can use the general solution of the logistic growth equation to make predictions about a population governed by logistic growth. Let's see how." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180792 #edb/ref 1065 981 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180792 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "7ba42b62-b5a3-5c54-8a92-9ce67945fd36" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180792 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184177 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050162 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184177 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050277 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184177 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186184535 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184177 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186184796 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184177 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding Tangent Lines to Polar Curves" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184177 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186184078 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184177 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "2216cdfc-7f2a-5c53-b10b-2d66f35fbf32" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184215 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186184437 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184215 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186184536 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184215 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding Normal Lines to Polar Curves" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184215 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186184120 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184215 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "3e95ede7-306b-55e1-9245-7493f0afb514" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184419 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186184944 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184419 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186185001 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184419 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186185006 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184419 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184419 #edb/ref 1045 "Tangent and Normal Lines to Polar Curves" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184419 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186185006 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184419 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049327 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184419 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "af91633b-288c-52bd-a641-7172621608f6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184944 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186185001 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184944 #edb/ref 1061 2206 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184944 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a0ead2f8-7726-5a22-ad4d-582c7b4b8968" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184944 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186184177 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185001 #edb/ref 1061 37487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185001 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "e6c2d34a-6683-50c4-a82e-b71e985d4ed8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185001 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186184215 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185006 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186184944 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185006 #edb/ref 1061 37501 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185006 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a1e51e4b-71cf-5749-aae1-c001b3a5a59b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185006 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186185342 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185342 #edb/ref 1064 "![](images/a0/a0b4ad67ea80f6d6eec3a15b3df18d551d84263c7424405588bd15162ef4e7c2.png)\n\nThe polar curve shown above is defined as\n\n$$\nr(\\theta) = 4 - 3\\cos \\theta, \\qquad 0\\leq \\theta < 2\\pi\n$$\n\nWe have\n\n$$\n\\theta = \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\n$$\n\nat the point $P$. The tangent to the curve at $P$ is shown in the diagram.\n\nTo find the equation of the tangent line to the curve at $P$, we follow four steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculate the derivative\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}\n$$\n\nUsing techniques that we've previously discussed, we can show that\n\n$$\ny^{′}(θ) = 4\\cos θ - 3\\cos 2θ, x^{′}(θ) = 3\\sin 2θ - 4\\sin θ\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can find\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}\n$$\n\nas follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dy}{dx} &= (y^{′}(θ))/(x^{′}(θ)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{4\\cos θ - 3\\cos 2θ}{3\\sin 2θ - 4\\sin θ}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find the slope of the tangent at $P$.\n\nThe slope of the tangent line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm &= \\left.\\frac{dy}{dx}\\right|_{θ = π/2} \\\\\n&= (4\\cos (\\frac{π}{2}) - 3\\cos (2 \\cdot \\frac{π}{2}))/(3\\sin (2 \\cdot \\frac{π}{2}) - 4\\sin (\\frac{π}{2})) \\\\\n&= (4\\cos (\\frac{π}{2}) - 3\\cos (π))/(3\\sin (π) - 4\\sin (\\frac{π}{2})) \\\\\n&= \\frac{0 + 3}{0 - 4} \\\\\n&=-\\frac{3}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Find the Cartesian coordinates of $P$.\n\nWe compute the $xy$-coordinates of $P$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{1} &= r\\cos θ \\\\\n&= (4 - 3\\cos θ)\\cos θ \\\\\n&= (4 - 3\\cos (\\frac{π}{2}))\\cos (\\frac{π}{2}) \\\\\n&= 0 \\\\\ny_{1} &= r\\sin θ \\\\\n&= (4 - 3\\cos θ)\\sin θ \\\\\n&= (4 - 3\\cos (\\frac{π}{2}))\\sin (\\frac{π}{2}) \\\\\n&= 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of $P$ are\n\n$$\n\\left(0, 4 \\right)\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Using point-slope form, find the equation of the tangent.\n\nWe find the equation of the tangent as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny - y_{1} &= m(x - x_{1}) \\\\\ny - 4 &= -\\frac{3}{4} \\cdot (x - 0) \\\\\ny &= -\\frac{3}{4}x + 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185342 #edb/ref 1065 6186 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185342 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "febc7b49-9662-5e78-a7a5-cbe9b1f596ba" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185342 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185532 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186500495 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185532 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186500566 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185532 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186500673 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185532 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186500728 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185532 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186500992 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185532 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185532 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185532 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186500992 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185532 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "9e2c35a2-00a1-5395-9ca6-d665e10ade6e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188257 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048214 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188257 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050288 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188257 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050411 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188257 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186188542 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188257 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186188543 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188257 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186373820 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188257 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186373827 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188257 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186373834 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188257 #edb/ref 103