ating Coordinate Plane Problems Into Differential Equations" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188292 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186187996 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188292 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "474f9c82-4ae7-58e0-9457-6bda9cab8973" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188455 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186189054 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188455 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186189104 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188455 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186189105 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188455 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186189107 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188455 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186189108 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188455 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186189245 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188455 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188455 #edb/ref 1045 "Further Modeling With First-Order ODEs" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188455 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186189104 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188455 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049739 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188455 #edb/ref 1050 840.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188455 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "cd68d011-8836-5efd-ab3b-ba09c700a3e9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188542 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186185532 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188542 #edb/ref 1069 "q-119161" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188542 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188542 #edb/ref 1071 "The area of a circle increases at a rate that's proportional to the square of its radius. Write down a differential equation describing the rate of change of the radius, $r(t),$ in this process. Assume that $K>0.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188542 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c8d1bea5-b961-5d22-9bfd-ba62f26db131" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188542 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*The **area** of a circle **increases** at a **rate** that's **proportional** to the **square of its radius**. *\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *area $A$ of a circle* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}$ *increases* at a rate that's *proportional* to the *square of its radius.* In other words,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=k{r}^{2}\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0.$\n\nWe wish to use this information to find an expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nThe area of a circle is given by \n\n$$\nA=\\pi {r}^{2}.\n$$\n\n \n\nDifferentiating this equation with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\pi {r}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =2\\pi r\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=k{r}^{2} \\\\ 2\\pi r\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=k{r}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{k{r}^{2}}{2\\pi r} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{kr}{2\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now introduce a new constant:\n\n$$\nK=\\frac{k}{2\\pi }>0\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, our equation becomes\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=Kr.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189054 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186189107 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189054 #edb/ref 1061 1853 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189054 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "da66f4c9-69eb-512a-b43d-9289b5f8fb5a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189054 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186188257 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189104 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186189105 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189104 #edb/ref 1061 38001 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189104 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "37206e3d-9800-567e-82f6-eed74a977f1f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189104 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186189375 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189105 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186189245 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189105 #edb/ref 1061 38002 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189105 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "9da430dc-8eca-5286-8278-c764eba32b5d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189105 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186188290 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189107 #edb/ref 1061 38012 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189107 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "53525e67-0e77-588f-88a9-1bda7f5964de" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189107 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186188291 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189108 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186189054 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189108 #edb/ref 1061 38016 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189108 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ccce909c-08ed-53ce-a30b-e357e8de3d59" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189108 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186188292 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189245 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186189108 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189245 #edb/ref 1061 53348 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189245 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d499c1d3-7173-5509-b128-c42561cce342" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189245 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186189439 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189375 #edb/ref 1064 "When constructing a differential equation to model a particular phenomenon, we sometimes combine several pieces of information to create the correct model.\n\nConsider the following situation:\n\n*The common cold virus spreads through a particular country. The rate at which the number of infected people increases is **proportional** to the **square of the time** $t$ (in days) since the outbreak started. \nAdditionally, $100$ infected people **recover from the virus each day**.*\n\nWe want to find a differential equation that models $P(t),$ the number of infected people. We note the following:\n\n- The statement describes the *rate* at which the *number of infected people* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d P}{\\textrm d t}.\n$$\n\n- The rate at which the number of infected people increases is **proportional** to the **square of the time** $t.$ Therefore, if $I(t)$ represents the rate at which infections increase, then\n\n$$\nI(t) = kt^2,\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is a positive constant.\n\n- Additionally, $100$ infected people **recover from the virus each day**. Therefore, if $D(t)$ represents the rate at which infections decrease, then\n\n$$\nD(t) = 100.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the differential equation that describes this phenomenon is as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t} & ={{k{t}^{2}-100}_{⏟}}_{I(t)-D(t)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlthough we introduced the functions $I(t)$ and $D(t)$ here, we can usually write down the appropriate differential equation straight away. Let's see an example." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189375 #edb/ref 1065 6270 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189375 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "8a7ad3b5-e4de-56c0-8fc9-8a9b2c976094" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189375 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189439 #edb/ref 1064 "Let's recall some basic facts from geometry in the $xy$-plane:\n\n- The distance between the point $(x,y)$ and the origin $(0,0)$ equals $\\sqrt{x^2+y^2}.$\n\n- The (smallest) distance between the point $(x,y)$ and the $x$-axis equals $|y|.$\n\n- Similarly, the (smallest) distance between the point $(x,y)$ and the $y$-axis equals $|x|.$\n\n- The slope of the line that passes through $(x,y)$ and the origin equals $\\dfrac{y}{x}.$\n\nWe sometimes need to use these basic facts when constructing differential equations described in a geometric context. \n\nLet's see an example." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189439 #edb/ref 1065 8152 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189439 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "b03d6daa-af90-5f14-bf0f-929da8526673" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189439 #edb/ref 1067 "Recalling Some Facts From Elementary Geometry" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233443 #edb/ref 1069 "q-138998" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233443 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233443 #edb/ref 1071 "Calculate $L={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{{a}_{n}}$ for the sequence ${a}_{n}=\\frac{3{n}^{n}}{(1+n)^{n}}.$ According to the root test, does ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ converge or diverge?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233443 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "16eb9f4b-4d3a-531a-a851-09494af3ba39" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233443 #edb/ref 1074 "Suppose we have the series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ such that ${a}_{n}\\ge 0$ for all $n\\ge 1,$ and that $L$ is defined as  \n\n$$\nL={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{{a}_{n}}={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{({a}_{n})}^{1/n}.\n$$\n\n \n\nThen, the root test states the following:\n\n- If $L<1$, the series converges.\n\n- If $L>1$ or $L=\\infty $, the series diverges.\n\n- If $L=1$, the root test is inconclusive.\n\nWe have the sequence ${a}_{n},$ defined as\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{3{n}^{n}}{(1+n)^{n}}.\n$$\n\nTo apply the root test, we find the limit of $\\sqrt[n]{{a}_{n}}$ as $n\\to \\infty :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{{a}_{n}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{({a}_{n})}^{1/n} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{3{n}^{n}}{(1+n)^{n}})}^{1/n} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\sqrt[n]{3}\\,n}{1+n} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\sqrt[n]{3}\\,n}{n(\\frac{1}{n}+1)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\sqrt[n]{3}}{(\\frac{1}{n}+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{0+1} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we used the fact that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{3}=1.\n$$\n\nSince $L=1,$ the root test gives no conclusion." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234032 #edb/ref 1069 "q-40691" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234032 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234032 #edb/ref 1071 "$2.3\\overset{―}{43}=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234032 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7b297bef-d86e-59df-8ce3-4a3826383e59" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234032 #edb/ref 1074 "The given repeating decimal can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2.3\\overset{―}{43} & =2.343\\,43... \\\\  & =2.3+0.043+0.000\\,43+0.000\\,004\\,3+… \\\\  & =2.3+{{\\frac{43}{1\\,000}+\\frac{43}{100\\,000}+\\frac{43}{10\\,000\\,000}+⋯.}_{⏟}}_{\\text{geometric series}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that we have a geometric series where the first term is ${a}_{1}=\\frac{43}{1\\,000}$ and the common ratio is $\\frac{1}{100}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2.3\\overset{―}{43} & =2.3+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{1}{r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =2.3+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{43}{1\\,000}{(\\frac{1}{100})}^{n-1} \\\\  & =2.3+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{43\\cdot 100}{1\\,000}{(\\frac{1}{100})}^{n} \\\\  & =2.3+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{43}{10}{(\\frac{1}{100})}^{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234373 #edb/ref 1069 "q-75637" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234373 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234373 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the positive infinite geometric sequence that has a first term equal to $56$ and a third term equal to $\\frac{7}{8}.$ What is the sum to infinity of the terms of this sequence?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234373 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "02b30695-cbca-5de7-a2d0-90b3f98db6af" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234373 #edb/ref 1074 "The sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of a geometric series is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1,\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term and $r$ is the common ratio.\n\nFrom the question statement we have ${a}_{1}=56$ and ${a}_{3}=\\frac{7}{8}.$ We can find the common ratio using the following formula:\n\n$$\n{r}^{n-m}=\\frac{{a}_{n}}{{a}_{m}}\n$$\n\nSubstituting in the known information into the above gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{3-1} & =\\frac{(\\frac{7}{8})}{56} \\\\ {r}^{2} & =(\\frac{7}{8})(\\frac{1}{56}) \\\\ {r}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{64} \\\\ r & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{64}} \\\\ r & =\\frac{1}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we have taken the positive square root since we're told that the sequence is positive.\n\nSo, using the formula, we find that the sum to infinity is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{\\infty } & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{56}{(1-\\frac{1}{8})} \\\\  & =\\frac{56}{(\\frac{7}{8})} \\\\  & =56(\\frac{8}{7}) \\\\  & =64.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256380 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186462736 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186256380 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186462737 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186256380 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186462738 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186256380 #edb/ref 1069 "q-120282" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256380 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256380 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/e9/e90d9a160db2c75091253eaa8a8c815e92c45f988841683f5a4d18ca6a6ea7db.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n- ${y}^{′}=-x{y}^{2}$\n- ${y}^{′}=-\\frac{y}{x}$\n- ${y}^{′}=\\frac{x}{y}$\n\nSelect all that apply." #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186256380 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "69c58940-535c-5bff-aff2-b382e99c5568" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256380 #edb/ref 1074 "![](images/54/54932bcffbbe793d6e1f730e65570b19cfc3603ddb62eaf0a63ef2e5b0705979.png)\n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- For $y\\ne 0,$ the slopes of the slope field become horizontal at $x=0$ only.\n\n- For $x\\ne 0,$ the slopes of the slope field become vertical as $y\\to 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- Equation I does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side of ${y}^{′}=-x{y}^{2}$ evaluates to zero both on $x=0$ and $y=0.$\n\n- Equation II does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side of ${y}^{′}=-\\frac{y}{x}$ does not evaluate to zero on $x=0.$\n\n- Equation III gives the required behavior. For $y\\ne 0,$ the right-hand side of ${y}^{′}=\\frac{x}{y}$ evaluates to zero on $x=0$ only, and for $x\\ne 0,$ we have \n\n$$\n|{y}^{′}|=|\\frac{x}{y}|\\to \\infty \\,\\text{as}\\,y\\to 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186339773 #edb/ref 1080 "x-9\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3x-2|+C" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186339773 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186339773 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "48658c86-5fff-5d1e-ad97-ca9615485d8d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186340399 #edb/ref 1080 "3x+9\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3x-2|+C" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186340399 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186340399 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "8d8c23eb-6ff2-5bd1-ab4a-f009409818c7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186340655 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{x}{3}+\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3x-2|+C" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186340655 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186340655 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "a839708e-6d86-5a57-b693-6f0e21e650eb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186340966 #edb/ref 1080 "x-\\frac{1}{9}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3x-2|+C" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186340966 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186340966 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "ced86a89-239e-50b2-894e-628af504a0d4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186341071 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{x}{3}-\\frac{1}{9}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3x-2|+C" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186341071 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186341071 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "daa4acb0-2d88-582f-a718-be7e56ba1265" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186341797 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186339773 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186341797 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186340399 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186341797 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186340655 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186341797 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186340966 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186341797 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186341071 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186341797 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186341797 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186341797 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186341071 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186341797 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "ea2bfd72-4857-53aa-b6a7-f5d94cbdaa7f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186341961 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341797 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186341961 #edb/ref 1069 "q-73391" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186341961 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186341961 #edb/ref 1071 "Calculate $\\int \\frac{x-1}{3x-2}\\,\\text{d}x.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186341961 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "40d2e0b8-4256-5b72-ae20-79f2c7d72c06" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186341961 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we pull out the factor of $3$ from the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{x-1}{3x-2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{x-1}{3(x-\\frac{2}{3})}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int \\frac{x-1}{(x-\\frac{2}{3})}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we draw up our synthetic division table to decompose the integrand into its quotient and remainder.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & x & {x}^{0} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} & 1 & -1 \\\\  & ↓ & \\frac{2}{3} \\\\  & 1 & -\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo from the synthetic division, we get that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-1}{(x-\\frac{2}{3})} & =1+\\frac{(-\\frac{1}{3})}{(x-\\frac{2}{3})} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{3(x-\\frac{2}{3})} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{3x-2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{x-1}{3x-2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int (1-\\frac{1}{3x-2})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\int \\text{d}x-\\int \\frac{1}{3x-2}\\,\\text{d}x] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[x-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3x-2|]+C \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{3}-\\frac{1}{9}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3x-2|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186349187 #edb/ref 1080 "-(2{e}^{2\\pi }+5)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186349187 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186349187 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "5180e91d-b92b-505b-ba26-cfc2c02ae8d3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186349273 #edb/ref 1080 "{e}^{2\\pi }+1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186349273 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186349273 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "59b0c393-ff53-5296-86d7-57e19a153481" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186349724 #edb/ref 1080 "2{e}^{2\\pi }+3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186349724 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186349724 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "8dfce7d1-3045-5f79-9f67-af6e541e7d40" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186349880 #edb/ref 1080 "-\\frac{2}{5}({e}^{2\\pi }+1)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186349880 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186349880 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "a1611af0-6b4b-511c-bdc0-8ccfe63b5718" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186350198 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{1}{5}({e}^{2\\pi }-1)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186350198 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186350198 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "c6f0ee9b-6deb-5faa-8e4b-9fac96efdbec" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351104 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186349187 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351104 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186349273 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351104 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186349724 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351104 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186349880 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351104 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186350198 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351104 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351104 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351104 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186349880 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351104 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "e2580160-8816-5519-b6e4-1d9a9bef341c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351476 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186351104 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351476 #edb/ref 1069 "q-83012" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351476 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351476 #edb/ref 1071 "Evaluate ${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351476 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "fbc072d0-2ec3-57b6-ba23-0219f96efe5d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351476 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's denote the indefinite integral by $I=\\int {e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.$\n\nTo use integration by parts, let's suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={e}^{2x}\\, & ⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2{e}^{2x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\int {e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\int (\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)(2{e}^{2x})\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-2\\int {e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-2J.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, we can use integration by parts to evaluate\n\n$$\nJ=\\int {e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo do this, let's suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={e}^{2x}\\, & ⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2{e}^{2x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}J & =\\int {e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{2x}(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-\\int (-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)(2{e}^{2x})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+2\\int {e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+2I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, substituting this into our equation for $I,$ we can subsequently solve for $I:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & ={e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-2J \\\\ I & ={e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-2(-{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+2I) \\\\ I & ={e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-4I \\\\ 5I & ={e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ I & =\\frac{{e}^{2x}}{5}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate our definite integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =(\\frac{{e}^{2x}}{5}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)){|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2\\pi }}{5}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi ))-\\frac{{e}^{2(0)}}{5}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2{e}^{2\\pi }}{5}-\\frac{2}{5} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{5}({e}^{2\\pi }+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399384 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186399385 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399384 #edb/ref 1069 "q-103125" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399384 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399384 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the radius of convergence $R$ and interval of convergence $I$ for the series\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(x+5)^{n}}{n(n+1)}\\,.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399384 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1c3c2591-89b8-5e94-a7b0-e02f5b424693" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399384 #edb/ref 1074 "To apply the ratio test, we define ${a}_{n}=\\frac{(x+5)^{n}}{n(n+1)}.$ Then, we calculate the ratio of two successive terms and take the limit as $n\\to \\infty :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{(x+5)^{n+1}}{(n+1)(n+2)}\\cdot \\frac{n(n+1)}{(x+5)^{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{n(x+5)}{n+2}| \\\\  & =|x+5|{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{n}{n+2} \\\\  & =|x+5|{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }(\\frac{1}{(1+\\frac{2}{n})}) \\\\  & =|x+5|\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =|x+5|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe series converges if $L<1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|x+5| & <1 \\\\ -1<x+5 & <1 \\\\ -6<x & <-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the radius of convergence is $R=1,$ and the series converges for any $x$ that lies in the open interval $(-6,-4).$\n\nThe ratio test gives no conclusion about the convergence of the series when $L=1.$ So, when $|x+5|=1,$ we do not know if the series converges. This means that the endpoints $x=-6$ and $x=-4$ need to be investigated separately using another test for convergence.\n\n- When $x=-6$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(x+5)^{n}}{n(n+1)} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-6+5)^{n}}{n(n+1)} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}+n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is convergent by the alternating series test.\n\n- When $x=-4$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(x+5)^{n}}{n(n+1)} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-4+5)^{n}}{n(n+1)} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{1}^{n}}{{n}^{2}+n} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}+n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing the above series with  ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}},$\nwe see that the series is convergent by the comparison test.\n\nSo the interval of convergence is $I=[-6,-4].$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399385 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399386 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399385 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399387 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399385 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399388 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399385 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399389 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399385 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399390 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399385 #ed